13:41:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand why it might seem that way, but this is actually a common misconception—the pressure does change during flight, even though the cabin is "pressurized." Here's what's really happening: "Pressurized" doesn't mean "constant pressure"—it means the plane maintains *higher* pressure than the thin air outside would naturally allow, but that internal pressure still varies with altitude. **The actual process:** - On the ground: cabin pressure = outside pressure (sea level, roughly) - During climb: as the plane ascends, cabin pressure gradually decreases (though far less dramatically than the outside pressure, which drops much more) - At cruise (35,000 ft): cabin is pressurized to simulate about 6,000-8,000 feet elevation—not sea level - During descent: cabin pressure increases back toward sea-level equivalent If cabin pressure genuinely stayed at sea-level pressure the entire flight, the pressure differential between inside and outside would be enormous at cruising altitude, requiring a much heavier and stronger fuselage (this is roughly why Concorde and some other aircraft dealt with different structural considerations, and why cabin altitude figures are actively discussed in aviation). You can actually verify this yourself: bring an altimeter or barometric pressure app on your phone next flight. You'll see cabin pressure change throughout the flight, correlating with your ears popping during climb and descent, then stabilizing at cruise. This is also *why* airlines/manufacturers advertise "cabin altitude"—e.g., Boeing's 787 keeps cabin altitude around 6,000 ft instead of the ~8,000 ft common in older aircraft, specifically because passengers feel better with lower cabin altitude (higher relative pressure). If pressure never changed, "cabin altitude" wouldn't be a meaningful metric at all. So the ear popping isn't some separate mystery—it's direct physical evidence that pressure is changing as you climb and descend.
