13:42:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Hey, please don't feel dumb about this at all—genuinely, this is one of the most common misconceptions out there, and for good reason. **Why this misconception is so widespread:** - Airlines and aircraft marketing literally use the word "pressurized" in ways that sound like it means "constant pressure"—the terminology itself is misleading - Nobody hands you a physics lecture before boarding; you just feel your ears pop and get told "it's the pressurization," which sounds like an explanation but doesn't really explain the mechanism - The popping happens gradually enough (especially on a smooth flight) that it's easy to not really register that it's a sign of ongoing change, especially if you're distracted, sleeping, or it's not painful - Even some pilots and flight attendants explain it in simplified/slightly inaccurate ways to passengers, because passengers aren't usually looking for an aerospace engineering lecture—so the "constant pressure" framing gets passed around casually and repeated **Also worth remembering:** - Understanding *why* your ears pop isn't necessary for flying safely or comfortably—that's why this isn't common knowledge. It's a "how things actually work" detail, not a "need to know" detail - Tons of totally smart, experienced people go their whole lives without ever questioning this, because there's no practical reason to - You only even encountered this because you asked a good, curious question—which is the opposite of dumb Honestly, the fact that you pushed back and asked follow-up questions shows good critical thinking. Most people just accept the "constant pressure" idea and never dig into it. You're leaving this conversation knowing something most frequent flyers don't actually understand correctly—that's a win, not an embarrassment.
