13:43:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is actually a sharp observation, and it points to something real—but I think there's a slight mismatch in the timeline that's worth clarifying. **Your ears popping isn't just at the literal moments of takeoff and landing—it's during the whole climb and whole descent**, which are much longer than "short bursts": - **Climb phase:** Often takes 20-30 minutes (sometimes longer) to reach cruising altitude. Pressure is changing gradually the *entire* time, not just at the moment wheels leave the ground. - **Cruise phase:** Once at altitude (this is usually the bulk of the flight—could be hours), cabin pressure *does* level off and stay essentially constant. This is the part where you're right that pressure isn't changing much! - **Descent phase:** Similarly takes 20-30 minutes, with pressure gradually changing back the whole time, not just in the final seconds before landing. **So actually, your instinct is pointing at something true**: pressure *is* constant during the long cruise portion of the flight. Where the "constant the whole time" idea breaks down is that people tend to mentally lump "climb" and "descent" into quick moments ("takeoff" and "landing") when really those are extended phases where pressure is actively changing over many minutes. **Why it might feel like short bursts to you:** - Ear popping isn't necessarily painful or noticeable constantly—it can happen in small increments, sometimes without you consciously registering each one - You might only *notice* it acutely a couple times (like at the moment of most rapid pressure change), even though smaller equalizations are happening throughout climb/descent - Some people are just less sensitive to it except at certain pressure-change thresholds So to directly answer your challenge: you're right that it's not "constantly changing" in the sense of the *whole flight*—it changes specifically during climb and descent (which together might be 40-60 minutes of a flight), then holds steady at cruise. Your logic correctly identified that pressure isn't in constant flux the entire time—it's just that "takeoff and landing" undersells how long those transition phases actually are.
