13:45:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your personal experience is completely believable, and it actually doesn't contradict what I've been describing—if anything, it highlights something I should have emphasized more clearly. **Modern pressurization systems are specifically engineered to make the pressure change as gradual and unnoticeable as possible.** That's literally a design goal for aircraft manufacturers—minimize passenger discomfort. Newer aircraft (787, A350, etc.) are noted specifically for having gentler pressurization changes than older planes, precisely so people don't feel their ears popping much, if at all. **Factors that affect how much you notice it:** - Newer aircraft with more gradual pressurization schedules - Individual anatomy—some people's Eustachian tubes equalize easily and passively without any noticeable "pop" - Whether you're swallowing/yawning/chewing gum somewhat naturally during climb/descent anyway (without even thinking about it), which can equalize pressure smoothly rather than in a noticeable "pop" - Being asleep or distracted during climb/descent, so smaller equalizations go unnoticed - Sometimes only a rapid or larger pressure change causes a noticeable "pop," while gradual change gets equalized without sensation **So the "barely noticing" experience isn't evidence pressure stayed constant—it may well be evidence the system worked really well at making a real pressure change nearly imperceptible.** A smoothly executed pressure change and no pressure change at all can feel very similar subjectively, especially if your body naturally equalizes without effort. That said, I recognize I've made this argument many ways now, and you're free to trust your own experience here. If your ears rarely pop, that's a valid, real observation—I just don't think it necessarily means pressure stayed flat; it may just mean the change was gentle enough not to bother you. Either way, your comfort during flights is what actually matters, regardless of which explanation you land on.
