13:46:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No, that's not quite right, and I want to clear this up clearly because I don't want to leave you with an alarming, inaccurate idea. **The plane is pressurized the entire flight, including during climb.** "Pressurized" starts working right from taxi/takeoff. What changes is the *target* cabin altitude the system is aiming for as the plane climbs—not whether pressurization is happening at all. Think of it like this: - **Ground:** Cabin pressure ≈ outside pressure (both at sea-level-equivalent) - **Climb:** System is actively pressurizing the whole time—keeping cabin pressure *much* higher than the rapidly-thinning outside air—while gradually allowing cabin altitude to rise toward its cruise target (usually 6,000-8,000 ft equivalent) - **Cruise:** System holds cabin pressure steady at that target - **Descent:** System continues pressurizing, gradually lowering cabin altitude back toward sea-level equivalent for landing At every single point in this process, the cabin is significantly higher pressure than the outside air at that altitude. At 35,000 feet outside, unprotected exposure would cause you to pass out in seconds—but the cabin the whole time is kept at a safe, breathable pressure equivalent to something like 6,000-8,000 ft, not "unpressurized" or "not yet pressurized." So there's no scary gap where the plane "isn't properly pressurized yet"—it's continuously, actively pressurized from wheels-up to wheels-down. The system is just adjusting the *level* it's targeting as altitude changes, similar to how a thermostat is always "heating-capable" even as it adjusts the target temperature throughout the day. Nothing alarming here—just wanted to make sure I didn't leave you with the wrong impression on that specific point.
