13:46:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I think this is where the specific wording is creating confusion, so let me try to isolate the actual logical step: **"Pressurized flight" is a category describing a *type of system in use*, not a claim about a single unchanging PSI value throughout.** It's similar to how "air conditioned building" doesn't mean the temperature is locked at exactly 68°F the second you walk in and the second before you leave, with zero fluctuation in between—it means the AC system is active and controlling the temperature, even as the target might shift (cooler at 2pm on a hot day, warmer overnight, etc.). The building doesn't stop being "air conditioned" just because the temperature reading changes throughout the day—it's air conditioned because the system is actively managing it. Similarly, "pressurized" describes that there's an active system maintaining breathable pressure (as opposed to the alternative—an unpressurized aircraft where cabin pressure would just match brutal, unbreathable outside pressure at altitude). It doesn't mean the specific number is frozen from gate to gate. So: **every moment of the flight is pressurized** (system active, breathable air maintained)—but that doesn't logically require **every moment being at the identical pressure value.** Those are two different claims, and the category name only makes the first claim, not the second. That's the actual distinction I think has been getting lost in a lot of our back-and-forth—hope this version lands more clearly.
