13:48:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really understandable worry, but I want to reassure you clearly: this is not a safety concern at all. Pressure changing during climb/descent is not some unexpected stress—it's something aircraft are specifically engineered and rigorously tested to handle as completely normal, routine operation. **Some concrete reassurance:** - **This happens on every single commercial flight, every day, for decades.** Aircraft fuselages are specifically designed for repeated pressurization/depressurization cycles—it's one of the most well-understood and tested aspects of aircraft engineering, not some overlooked risk. - **"Pressure cycling" is a known, expected, engineered-for phenomenon.** Aircraft manufacturers test fuselages through tens of thousands of simulated pressurization cycles (far more than a plane will ever see in its real operational life) before the aircraft type is ever certified to fly passengers. - **Planes are inspected specifically with this in mind.** Maintenance schedules include checks for metal fatigue related to pressurization cycles, and aircraft are retired or undergo major overhauls specifically factoring in the number of pressurization cycles they've experienced—this is a known, managed, accounted-for part of aviation safety, not a hidden danger. - **The gradual change during climb/descent is actually easier on the structure than a sudden, dramatic pressure difference would be.** Rapid, uncontrolled pressure change would be the scary version. Gradual, controlled change—which is what actually happens—is specifically the gentle, engineered approach designed to minimize stress on the structure and on your body (ears). So if anything, you can flip the framing: the fact that pressure gradually changes (rather than the cabin trying to instantly match sea-level or instantly matching high-altitude thin air) is *part of* how the system protects the aircraft and you—not evidence of extra risk. Genuinely, there's nothing here that should add to flight anxiety. This is about as well-tested and routine as aviation engineering gets.
